  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE...Localized urban flash flooding possible this morning \nas warm front lifts north.\n\nA warm front is lifting across the Mid-Atlantic this morning.\n12Z analysis shows a small meso-low feature near Madison VA, and\nthis feature will likely drift northeastward just northwest of\nUS-29 toward Baltimore's northwest suburbs through midday.\n\nOn radar and satellite, this feature has the appearance of an\noccluded low. The pseudo \"triple point\" east of DC toward\nAnnapolis is where convergence is strongest on the mesoscale,\noutside of the low moving from near Madison to Culpeper.\n\nThe highest PW axis is overhead this morning, so anything that\ndevelops as subtle daytime heating commences will be capable of\nvery heavy rainfall rates. Slow storm motions/training are\npossible as this weak low moves overhead. Forcing for ascent via\nmoisture flux has ceased, so further development will rely on\nsubtle convergence axes and/or heating of an extremely humid\nairmass through limited cloud breaks.\n\nHave cancelled the watch as convergence continues to decrease\nfrom DC south and west. Meanwhile, convergence and CAPE will\nincrease to the east and northeast of DC, in the Baltimore and\nAnnapolis area through this afternoon. The last few runs of the\nHRRR have rather stubborn recurring activity in this area into \nthe afternoon, so the watch was expanded to include that area.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Daily shower and thunderstorm chances are expected \nthrough early next week.\n\nIn the wake of what morning convection materializes, the models do \nshow a brief lull in the action. The northward pushing warm front \neventually stalls nearby before shearing out. Likewise, the upper \nlow across the Mid-South region begins to fill although the \nreflection of this circulation still shows up across southern West \nVirginia. \n\nA deep southerly flow will continue around high pressure over the \nAtlantic, ushering plenty of moisture. PWATS will be near or greater \nthan 2\" in the valleys with 1-2\" in the mountains, which is about 1-\n2 SD above climo. A surface trough and shortwave energy will trigger \nscattered showers/thunderstorms, but it should not be a washout \nduring the afternoon/evening. The main threat will be brief heavy \ndownpours and gusty winds. However, DCAPE and shear are on the lower \nside, so the probability of severe storms is lower. If some storms \ntrain over the same area, localized flash flooding can occur. The \npattern does favor the possibility for training convection (cloud \nlayer winds nearly parallel to low-level wind), but the coverage \nshould be isolated. \n\nA similar pattern will hold on for Thursday and Friday while the \nsubtropical jet strengthens to the north and west. Therefore, more \nshowers and thunderstorms are likely along with the threat for \nisolated instances of flash flooding and locally damaging wind \ngusts. Hotter conditions may cause relatively steeper low-level \nlapse rates and the wind field strengthens a bit (especially \nFriday). \n\nA quasi-zonal flow aloft will develop for the weekend into next \nweek, bringing more seasonably hot and humid conditions along with \ndaily chances for showers and thunderstorms.",
